
================================================================================================
mi_memory_weight_w_2026 -- can physics fix the memory weight w?
================================================================================================

================================================================================================
T1. WHAT PHYSICAL PROCESS SETS A MEMORY KERNEL? -- timescale ledger
================================================================================================
  TARGET  tau_mem = 2c/a0 = 203.0 Gyr (canonical a0=9.36e-11)
                          = 168.1 Gyr (alternative a0=1.13e-10)
  [OK  ] canonical 2c/a0 reproduces the quoted 203 Gyr (203.0)
  [OK  ] alternative 2c/a0 reproduces the quoted 168 Gyr (168.1)

  de Sitter Hubble time 1/H_Lambda = 17.53 Gyr  (H_L = 1.807e-18 1/s)

  candidate process                                timescale   ratio to 2c/a0  dissip?
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  dS horizon light-crossing r_H/c = 1/H_L           17.5 Gyr          0.08637       no
      the ONE natural horizon time
  dS quasinormal relaxation 1/(n H_L), n>=1         17.5 Gyr          0.08637      YES
      tower goes DOWN from 1/H_L, never up
  dS Gibbons-Hawking 1/T_dS = 2pi/H_L                110 Gyr           0.5427      YES
      thermal correlation time
  Unruh bath at a=a0: 1/T_U = 2pi c/a0               638 Gyr            3.142      YES
      same a0 scale, coefficient 2pi
  electron radiation reaction 2e^2/3mc^3          6.27e-24 s        9.782e-43      YES
      Abraham-Lorentz-Dirac
  GW radiation reaction (J0737 inspiral)           0.086 Gyr        0.0004237      YES
      ~86 Myr, system-dependent
  condensate/aether relaxation                            --               --      YES
      set by the condensate scale M -- FREE, must be tuned to a0

  READING -- the dimensional degeneracy that makes this ledger nearly vacuous:
  a0 and c admit EXACTLY ONE timescale, c/a0 = 101.5 Gyr. So ANY
  candidate whose scale is set by a0 lands within an O(1) factor of 2c/a0
  AUTOMATICALLY, carrying ZERO evidential weight. Only a process predicting the
  COEFFICIENT (exactly 2) to real precision would count. None below does:
    dS horizon      : 2c/a0 = 11.58 x (1/H_L)   -- off by 11.6x
    dS thermal      : 2c/a0 = 1.843 x (2pi/H_L)
    Unruh at a0     : 2c/a0 = 0.3183 x (2pi c/a0) = (1/pi) x
  Two of these sit within a factor ~2 of the target. That is what dimensional
  degeneracy GUARANTEES, not evidence. Radiation reaction misses by ~40 orders.

  A SECOND, STRONGER OBSTRUCTION -- the no-dissipation constraint:
  Im K == 0 identically under the closure (argument |a|^2 >= 0, cut needs z <= -1/4),
  and any secular drift is excluded at 8.5 sigma by PSR J0737-3039 timing. But every
  bath-sourced memory kernel is DISSIPATIVE by the fluctuation-dissipation theorem:
  a bath that supplies memory necessarily supplies Im chi != 0. Every 'YES' in the
  dissip? column above is therefore excluded on ANALYTIC CHARACTER, independently of
  its timescale. The only non-dissipative entry is the bare horizon light-crossing
  time, which misses by 11.6x. => NO candidate on this list can source w.

================================================================================================
T2. IS THE BRANCH POINT A PHYSICAL MEMORY TIME, OR A PARAMETRISATION ARTEFACT?
================================================================================================
  The framework's law, written TWO equivalent ways (same physics, exactly):
    (A) g_obs = g_bar * nu(y),  y = g_bar/a0,  nu = sqrt(1 + 1/y)
    (B) g_bar = g_obs * mu(x),  x = g_obs/a0,  mu = (sqrt(4*x**2 + 1) - 1)/(2*x)
  [OK  ] (A) is g_obs^2 = g_bar^2 + a0 g_bar  (residual 0)
  [OK  ] (B) inverts (A) exactly (residual 0)
  [OK  ] identity K(x^2) = mu_fw(x) as given

  Nearest singularity of each form, in its own (acceleration/a0) variable:
    (B) mu(x): 1+4x^2 = 0 -> x = [-I/2, I/2], |x| = 0.5
    (A) nu(y): 1+1/y = 0 -> y = [-1], |y| = 1.0

    tau from (B) = c/(a0 |x|) = 203.0 Gyr   <-- this is the quoted 2c/a0
    tau from (A) = c/(a0 |y|) = 101.5 Gyr
  [OK  ] (B) reproduces 203 Gyr (203.0)
  [OK  ] (A) gives EXACTLY HALF of (B): ratio 0.500000

  >>> The SAME LAW gives two different 'memory times', differing by exactly 2,
      depending only on whether you parametrise by g_bar or by g_obs. A physical
      relaxation time cannot depend on that bookkeeping choice.

  Now across interpolation functions that all fit the RAR comparably well:
  interpolation function                                |sing|   tau (Gyr)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  framework / Milgrom 1999 mu=(sqrt(1+4x^2)-1)/2x          0.5         203   (branch)
  standard      mu = x/sqrt(1+x^2)                           1         101   (branch at x=+-i)
  simple        mu = x/(1+x)                                 1         101   (pole at x=-1)
  McGaugh RAR   nu = 1/(1-exp(-sqrt(y)))                 39.48        2.57   (poles at y=-4 pi^2 k^2)

  SPREAD across RAR-acceptable functions: factor 79.0 (2.6 - 203.0 Gyr)
  The McGaugh RAR function additionally has a sqrt branch point AT y=0, which would
  give an UNBOUNDED 'memory time'. So the answer is not merely different, it is
  not even finite for a function that fits the same data.

  DECISIVE, and internal to the framework: under the closure the operator argument
  is |a|^2 >= 0. The branch point sits at |a|^2 = -a0^2/4, i.e. at an IMAGINARY
  acceleration, on a part of the domain the dynamics PROVABLY never visits
  (that is exactly why Im K == 0 identically). Reading a relaxation time off a
  singularity the theory can never reach requires two further unmotivated steps:
  accepting a negative squared argument, and converting acceleration -> frequency
  via omega = a/c, which appears nowhere in the closure.

  VERDICT (T2): tau_mem = 2c/a0 is a DIMENSIONAL RE-EXPRESSION OF THE ALGEBRAIC
  NUMBER 1/4 in sqrt(1+4z) -- a property of the calibrated functional form, not a
  physical memory timescale. It is parametrisation-dependent (factor 2), varies by
  a factor 79 across equally good fits, and lives off the physical domain.

================================================================================================
T3. DEEP-MOND SPACETIME SCALE INVARIANCE -- does it fix w?
================================================================================================
  Milgrom 2009 (ApJ 698, 1630; arXiv:0810.4065): the deep-MOND limit is invariant
  under (t, r) -> (lambda t, lambda r). From this symmetry follow asymptotically
  flat rotation curves, the BTFR and Faber-Jackson. The framework's DML
  g_obs = sqrt(a0 g_bar) has exactly this form, so it inherits the requirement.

  DML of the framework: K(z) -> sqrt(z) as z -> 0, so the DML law is x_w a = a_N.
  [OK  ] K(z)/sqrt(z) -> 1 as z->0 (got 1) => DML is x_w a = a_N

  Under (t,r)->(lambda t, lambda r):  r_lam(t) = lambda r(t/lambda), so
  a_lam(t) = a(t/lambda)/lambda  and  a_N -> a_N/lambda^2.
  Invariance of x_w a = a_N therefore REQUIRES  x_w[r_lam](tau) = x_w[r](tau/lam)/lam.
  Substituting s = lambda sigma in the convolution gives the functional equation
        lambda w(lambda s) = w(s)      for all lambda > 0.

  Numerical test on a NON-circular trajectory, lambda = 2.7, tau = 1.3:
  weight w                             x_w[r_lam](tau)   x_w[r](tau/lam)/lam   invariant?
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ULTRALOCAL  w = delta(s)                  0.53752248            0.53752248          YES
  ORBIT-AVERAGED (window = period)          0.45057500            0.45057500          YES
  fixed-width exponential (tau_mem)         0.45243300            0.45042739           NO
  homogeneous w ~ 1/s (unnormalisable)        0.48529075            0.47908956           NO
  [OK  ] ULTRALOCAL is DML scale-invariant
  [OK  ] ORBIT-AVERAGED is DML scale-invariant (window scales with orbit)
  [OK  ] FIXED-WIDTH kernel BREAKS DML scale invariance (as the functional equation demands)

  WHY the two named members both survive: delta satisfies lam*delta(lam s)=delta(s)
  identically, and the orbit-average window is not an external constant -- its width
  is the trajectory's own period, which rescales WITH the orbit. Both are scale-free.

  >>> CONSEQUENCE FOR THE 203 Gyr READING. A weight with a FIXED width tau_mem
      introduces a new dimensioned constant beyond a0 and breaks the very symmetry
      that delivers flat rotation curves and the BTFR. Milgrom's own construction
      (arXiv:2208.07073) avoids this by making his weight depend only on a RATIO of
      frequencies -- 'Only ratios of frequencies enter, and a0 remains the only new
      dimensioned constant.' The 203 Gyr kernel is exactly the forbidden kind.

  SECOND, INDEPENDENT PROBLEM with taking 203 Gyr seriously: it exceeds EVERY
  astrophysical dynamical time by orders of magnitude, so w would be effectively
  flat over every observable orbit:
    wide binary at 10 kAU        t_dyn ~ 1e+06 yr  ->  tau_mem / t_dyn = 2.03e+05 orbits
    MW vertical oscillation      t_dyn ~ 8e+07 yr  ->  tau_mem / t_dyn = 2.54e+03 orbits
    solar Galactic orbit         t_dyn ~ 2e+08 yr  ->  tau_mem / t_dyn = 923 orbits
    dwarf satellite orbit        t_dyn ~ 2e+09 yr  ->  tau_mem / t_dyn = 81.2 orbits
  i.e. the 203 Gyr reading does NOT leave the choice open -- it collapses the whole
  family onto the ORBIT-AVERAGED member for every system we can observe, and makes
  ULTRALOCAL unreachable. It is therefore not a neutral curiosity: it PREDICTS the
  -0.037 dex dispersion-support offset rather than zero. Testable, and it is the
  member that DML scale invariance penalises. The two readings cannot both be held.

