T4. OBSERVABLE LEVER -- how sharply does eccentricity separate the weightings?
================================================================================================
  [OK  ] INT (1+e cos)^2 dtheta = 2 pi (1 + e^2/2)  (got pi*(e**2 + 2))

  EXACT result: for a Kepler orbit of semi-major axis A, with a_circ = GM/A^2,
      sqrt(<|a|^2>_t) / a_circ = sqrt(e**2/2 + 1)/(1 - e**2)**(5/4)
  [OK  ] reduces to 1 at e=0 (all weightings agree)

       e   orbit-avg / a_circ      dex   ultralocal at apo   apo split (dex)
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
     0.0               1.0000    0.000              1.0000             0.000
     0.2               1.0628    0.026              0.6944             0.185
     0.4               1.2923    0.111              0.5102             0.404
     0.6               1.8976    0.278              0.3906             0.686
     0.8               4.1201    0.615              0.3086             1.125
     0.9               9.4492    0.975              0.2770             1.533

  Compare the framework's own dispersion-support test: 0.037 dex (= factor 1.089).
  At e = 0.6 the orbit-averaged/ultralocal split at apocentre is 0.69 dex,
  i.e. ~19x the lever of the circular-system test.

  DWARF-SATELLITE EFE, RESOLVED BY ORBITAL PHASE. Milgrom (arXiv:2208.07073) makes
  the same distinction explicitly: the EFE is set by a time-average <a_ex> under a
  memory weight, versus the MOMENTARY a_ex under ultralocal weighting.

    orbit spans r = 33 - 117 kpc; time-rms a_ex/a0 = 0.125
    In the EFE-dominated regime internal dynamics are Newtonian with G_eff =
    G/mu(a_ex/a0), so sigma^2 ~ 1/mu. Split as a function of CURRENT phase:

  current phase               r (kpc)  a_ex/a0 (momentary)  sigma_oa/sigma_ul     dex
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  at pericentre                    33                0.420              0.581   0.236
  mid-orbit                        60                0.231              0.748   0.126
  at apocentre                    117                0.118              1.025   0.011

  >>> IMPORTANT CORRECTION TO THE OBVIOUS OBSERVING STRATEGY. The split is NOT
      largest for the famous EFE dwarfs. An eccentric orbit spends most of its time
      near APOCENTRE, so the time-average is dominated by the apocentric field --
      which means at apocentre the orbit-averaged and momentary values nearly
      COINCIDE and the test has almost no power. Crater II and Antlia II are both
      near apocentre (Pace+ 2022), so the flagship EFE successes are the WRONG
      objects for this test. The discriminating power is concentrated in dwarfs
      currently near PERICENTRE, where the momentary field greatly exceeds the
      orbit average. That is a sharp, cheap selection rule for a DR4 sample.

  RANKING by lever and by whether the dominant systematic is COHERENT (bad) or
  RANDOM (good), and -- decisively -- whether it CORRELATES with the discriminant:

    1. MW satellite dwarfs NEAR PERICENTRE, phase-resolved EFE
       lever ~0.13-0.24 dex | systematic: random per object (small-N sigma, binaries)
       orbits known per object from Gaia; M/L does NOT correlate with orbital phase, so the coherent systematic is orthogonal to the discriminant; select near pericentre (apocentric dwarfs have ~no power); BUT tidal disturbance ALSO correlates with pericentre -- that confound is the main threat and must be modelled

    2. Eccentric wide binaries (2-30 kAU)
       lever ~0.2-0.9 dex | systematic: COHERENT (undetected triples; unknown e-distribution)
       largest raw lever, but e is unmeasurable per system at these separations, so the e-distribution prior is DEGENERATE with w itself; also sits in the omega_c gate dead zone

    3. MW vertical dynamics, population-differential
       lever ~0.05-0.2 dex | systematic: coherent MW potential -- but CANCELS between populations at fixed R
       different populations share a_ex and differ in vertical frequency; Milgrom flags exactly this case; a genuinely differential test

    4. Tidal streams (GD-1, Pal 5)
       lever ~0.1-0.3 dex | systematic: COHERENT (MW baryonic potential + progenitor orbit)
       precise geometry but the systematic is the model itself

    5. Infalling cluster members
       lever ~<0.1 dex | systematic: COHERENT (cluster mass modelling)
       weakest; MOND clusters already carry an unresolved central residual

================================================================================================
T5. IS w REALLY THE ENTIRE REMAINING FREEDOM? -- the composite-body test
================================================================================================
  Milgrom flagged this in the ORIGINAL 1994 paper (Annals of Physics 229, 384;
  astro-ph/9303012) and it is still open in 2022:
    "We cannot yet offer an example of a theory that fully accounts for the correct
     center-of-mass motion of composite objects: A composite body (e.g. a star),
     with constituents whose internal motion is Newtonian should still undergo a
     center-of-mass motion similar to that of a test particle, which may be well in
     the MOND regime."
  His fix needs FREQUENCY structure: high-frequency internal accelerations must be
  SUPPRESSED in the low-frequency (galactic) inertia. In 2208.07073 that is the
  weight theta(omega'/omega), which decays for omega' >> omega.

  Now test the framework's family against it. THE OBSTRUCTION IS POSITIVITY.
  Theorem 1 makes the argument exactly +|a|^2 (from u.u = -1), so for ANY normalised
  w >= 0:   if |a(tau)| >= A on supp(w), then x_w >= A/a0.  A positive weight cannot
  SUPPRESS a large internal acceleration -- averaging a positive quantity keeps it.

  Star constituent: |a_int| = 2.93e+12 a0 (solar surface gravity), |a_gal| = 0.5 a0

  weight w                                         x_w   mu_fw(x_w)    MOND?
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ULTRALOCAL delta (at generic phase)         1.53e+12     1.000000       NO
  [OK  ] ULTRALOCAL delta (at generic phase) leaves the constituent NEWTONIAN (mu=1.000000)
  ORBIT-AVERAGED over internal period         2.07e+12     1.000000       NO
  [OK  ] ORBIT-AVERAGED over internal period leaves the constituent NEWTONIAN (mu=1.000000)
  fixed-width kernel, any width               2.07e+12     1.000000       NO
  [OK  ] fixed-width kernel, any width leaves the constituent NEWTONIAN (mu=1.000000)

  Every member gives mu_fw ~ 1: the constituent is Newtonian, so the star's total
  inertia is Newtonian, so its centre of mass feels NO MOND in the galaxy -- and
  rotation curves, which are the framework's primary evidence, would be Newtonian.
  This is not a property of a bad choice of w. It follows from |a|^2 >= 0, i.e. from
  the SAME positivity (Theorem 1, u.u = -1) that gives Im K == 0. The framework's
  positivity-locked first moment has no suppression mechanism at all.

  THE FORK, both horns stated:
   (i)  If the law applies to every timelike worldline (as Theorem 1 states), then NO
        member of the w-family solves the composite-body problem. The freedom is in
        the wrong place: fixing w would not complete the inertia sector.
   (ii) If instead the law applies only to designated centre-of-mass worldlines, the
        theory needs an extra rule saying WHICH worldlines are fundamental (star?
        cloud? galaxy?). That rule is a new free choice, and a BIGGER one than w.
  Either way the brief's premise -- that w is the entire remaining freedom -- does
  not survive. Milgrom escapes only because his weight is a frequency RATIO acting
  on |a_hat(omega)|, which a positive time-domain weight on |a|^2 cannot reproduce.

================================================================================================
SUMMARY -- stated whatever the outcome
================================================================================================
  1. (T1) No enumerated physical process sets 2c/a0. Worse, the comparison is
     structurally uninformative: c/a0 is the ONLY time a0 and c can build, so any
     a0-scale process matches to O(1) automatically. And every bath-sourced kernel
     is dissipative, which the framework's own Im K == 0 (and the 8.5 sigma
     J0737-3039 bound) forbids. The candidate class is empty on analytic character.
  2. (T2) tau_mem = 2c/a0 is an ARTEFACT. It changes by exactly 2 under a pure
     re-parametrisation of the SAME law (nu-form vs mu-form), and by a factor
     79 across interpolation functions that fit the RAR comparably. The branch
     point also sits at an imaginary acceleration the dynamics provably never
     reaches. It should NOT be presented as a memory timescale.
  3. (T3) DML spacetime scale invariance (Milgrom 2009) is a REAL constraint: it
     forces lambda w(lambda s) = w(s). Ultralocal and orbit-averaged both pass;
     every FIXED-width kernel fails, including a 203 Gyr one. So w is narrowed to
     scale-free members, but NOT to a unique one.
  4. (T4) Eccentric systems beat the circular-system test by ~6-20x in lever, but
     ONLY near pericentre -- at apocentre the orbit average and the momentary value
     nearly coincide, so the famous EFE dwarfs have almost no power. w must be fixed
     by DATA, and the sharpest available are pericentric dwarf EFE measurements.
  5. (T5) w is NOT the entire remaining freedom. Positivity (|a|^2 >= 0, the same
     Theorem-1 positivity that gives Im K == 0) means NO member of the w-family
     suppresses a star's internal accelerations, so none solves the composite-body
     problem Milgrom named in 1994 and had not solved in 2022. Either the law fails
     for composite bodies, or it needs an extra rule naming which worldlines are
     fundamental -- a larger freedom than w itself.
================================================================================================
